g (which we just concluded it does), then the dog is round.\n\nTherefore, conditional 19\'s antecedent is satisfied, so the consequent must hold: the dog is round.\n\nBut wait, in the facts, the rabbit is round (fact 12).The dog\'s roundness is not mentioned.So according to conditional 19, the dog must be round.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the dog is round.\n\nNow, if the dog is round, then according to conditional 15, since round → rough, the dog is rough.\n\nThen, conditional 17 says that if something is rough, then it visits the lion.Since the dog is rough (from being round and conditional 15), then the dog must visit the lion.\n\nLooking at the facts, the lion visits the dog (fact 11), but does the dog visit the lion?The facts don\'t explicitly say that.However, according to conditional 17, since the dog is rough, it must visit the lion.Therefore, the dog visits the lion is true.\n\nWait, but the problem states the lion visits the dog (fact 11), but the dog visiting the lion is now inferred via the dog being rough.\n\nSo that\'s an additional fact to consider.\n\nNow, let\'s see if we can find any connection to the rabbit seeing the bald eagle.\n\nThe question is whether "the rabbit does not see the bald eagle" is true.So, we need to determine if the rabbit sees the bald eagle or not.\n\nLooking at the facts, there\'s no direct statement about the rabbit seeing anyone.The only "sees" is the dog seeing the lion (fact 5).\n\nAre there any conditionals that could lead us to conclude that the rabbit sees the bald eagle or not?\n\nLet me check conditional 18 again: If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog.\n\nWe know from conditional 21 that the rabbit needs the dog, but do we know if the dog needs the rabbit?The facts don\'t state that the dog needs the rabbit.The lion needs the dog (fact 8), but the dog\'s needs aren\'t specified.So unless there\'s another conditional that requires it, we can\'t assume 