uld trigger another conditional.\n\nLooking at condition 20: If something doesn\'t visit the rabbit, then rabbit doesn\'t need bald eagle.\n\nBut the rabbit not visiting the dog is the rabbit not going to the dog, not something not visiting the rabbit.\n\nSo that doesn\'t apply.\n\nAlternatively, the dog is not visited by the rabbit (fact13).\n\nWait, no, the rabbit visits the dog, so the dog isn\'t being visited by the rabbit.\n\nHmm, perhaps no other conditionals apply here.\n\nSo, after going through all the conditionals, I don\'t see any that directly link the rabbit not seeing the bald eagle.\n\nTherefore, the information given provides direct or indirect evidence that the rabbit does or does not see the bald eagle. Therefore, the answer should be known (A).\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nWait, the question is "Is the following statement true: The rabbit sees the bald eagle?" So if we can\'t prove it does or doesn\'t, then it\'s known.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s an implicit assumption.\n\nWait, in the facts, the bald eagle avoids the rabbit (fact4). Does avoiding imply not seeing? The problem doesn\'t say that avoiding means not seeing, so they\'re separate actions.\n\nTherefore, unless there\'s a conditional that connects avoiding to not seeing, which there isn\'t, we can assume that avoiding implies not seeing.\n\nThus, since there\'s information about the rabbit not seeing the bald eagle, the correct answer is not unknown (C).\n\nWait, but let me check again for any possible chain I might have missed.\n\nAnother thought: The rabbit\'s lack of needs. The rabbit doesn\'t need the bald eagle (from condition 20) and the dog (from condition 21).\n\nDoes not needing someone imply that they are not seen? Not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, the bald eagle doesn\'t need the lion (fact3). Does that imply the bald eagle doesn\'t see the lion? Not unless stated.\n\nSo no, needs don\'t imply not seeing.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is not unknown (