rel is red.\n14.The squirrel does not visit the lion.\n15.If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is green.\n16.If the mouse does not chase the lion, then the mouse needs the lion.\n17.If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel does not chase the lion, then the lion is green.\n18.If something is young, then it visits the bald eagle.\n19.If something visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel.\n20.If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young.\n21.If something needs the squirrel and it is not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n\nThe question is about the bald eagle\'s age (young or not).Let\'s see which of the statements might relate to that.\n\nLooking at statements 18, 19, and 20, these seem crucial because they involve "young" and "visits the bald eagle".\n\nStatement 18: If something is young, then it visits the bald eagle.So, being young implies visiting the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 19: If something visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel.So visiting the bald eagle leads the bald eagle to visit the squirrel.\n\nStatement 20: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young.Since the squirrel is red (from statement 13), this would mean that if something visits the squirrel, then that something is young.\n\nWait, let me parse that again.Statement 20 says: If (something visits the squirrel) AND (the squirrel does not chase the lion), then the lion is green.Wait, no, that\'s statement 17.Let me check again.\n\nWait, I might have misnumbered.Let me recheck the given statements:\n\nWait the user listed them as 1 to 21.Let me recount to confirm:\n\n1-14 are the base facts.Statements 15-21 are the conditional statements.Let me confirm:\n\n15.If something visits the mouse → mouse is green.\n\n16.If mouse doesn\'t chase lion → mouse needs lion.\n\n17.If something visits the squirrel AND squirrel doesn’t chase lion → lion is green.\n\n18.If something 