eate a contradiction.But then, also, he would be white (from point 9: nice and big → white).So he would be both red and white.Unless that\'s impossible, but the problem doesn\'t say colors can\'t coexist.So maybe that\'s allowed.But then, the problem might consider that being white and red would conflict, but unless stated, perhaps not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way.Let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says "All quiet things are big" (point 14).We have Dave is quiet, so he\'s big.Also, he\'s round.So he\'s big and round, so he can be white (from point 9).But if he\'s also red, then he would have two colors, but that\'s possible unless prohibited.However, the question is whether Dave is not red.The options are to say true, false, or unknown.\n\nWait, but perhaps there\'s a way to determine that he can\'t be red.Let\'s see.\n\nIf Dave is white, then can he also be red?The problem\'s rules don\'t say that a thing can\'t have both colors.So unless there\'s a rule that says, for example, if something is white, it can\'t be red, which there isn\'t.So maybe it\'s possible for him to be both, but perhaps there\'s another constraint.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s look at point 15: Young, quiet things are red.If Dave were young, then since he\'s quiet, he must be red.But if he\'s not young, then he doesn\'t have to be red.But can we know if he\'s young?\n\nDave\'s age isn\'t given directly except in the premises.The only mentions of young are Bob (young), Gary (young), and from Erin being big and red, so Erin is young.But Dave\'s age isn\'t stated.So unless there\'s a rule that makes him young, we can\'t assume.\n\nWait, but if he is big and white (from being nice and big), and big and white implies round (point 10).But he is round already, so that\'s okay.But being big and white doesn\'t make him young, unless there\'s a rule.Wait, point 10 is "If something is big and white then it is round."Which is satisfied because he is round.So that doesn\'t require him to