ut in the problem\'s context, perhaps it\'s okay).\n\nWait, the cow does see the squirrel (fact3), but the conditional\'s first part is "sees the cow".So if the cow is the "someone" in question, then does the cow see the cow?Well, perhaps that\'s not necessary.Maybe the conditional is referring to another animal.Let\'s see:\n\nThe cow chases the squirrel (fact1), and the cow sees the squirrel (fact3).But the conditional requires that the someone (the cow in this case) sees the cow (themselves?), and chases the squirrel, leading to seeing the rabbit.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the conditional is meant to be "if someone sees the cow and chases the squirrel, then they see the rabbit".So if the cow is the one doing the chasing, then the cow must see the cow (themselves) and chase the squirrel, so they must see the rabbit.Therefore, according to this, the cow must see the rabbit.\n\nBut is there any information about the cow seeing the rabbit?The problem doesn\'t state that.So according to this conditional, if the cow is indeed chasing the squirrel and seeing the cow (itself), then the cow must see the rabbit.Therefore, we can infer that the cow sees the rabbit.\n\nBut let\'s see if this is necessary.Let\'s not get bogged down here yet.Let\'s get back to the original question.The question is whether the cow does NOT chase the squirrel.But fact1 explicitly says the cow does chase the squirrel.Therefore, the statement in the question is the negation of that fact, so it should be false.\n\nHowever, maybe there\'s a contradiction in the given information that would invalidate fact1?Let me check for any contradictions.\n\nFirst, the cow is young (fact2).The conditional fourth says that if someone is young, they don\'t see the dog.Therefore, the cow does not see the dog, which is okay unless there\'s a fact contradicting that.The problem doesn\'t mention the cow seeing the dog, so no conflict there.\n\nThe squirrel is also young (fact11), so the squirrel doesn\'t see the dog ei