isn\'t mentioned as smart.)\n\n2.Big, blue things are round.(Anne is big but not mentioned as blue, so this might not apply to her.)\n\n3.If Gary is cold then Gary is smart.(Gary is already smart, but this is a conditional.)\n\n4.All blue, furry things are big.(If Anne were blue and furry, then she\'s big, which she is.But she\'s already big.Not sure if that affects.)\n\n5.If Gary is furry then Gary is blue.(Since Gary is smart, he\'s furry, so this would imply Gary is blue.Anne is warm and not furry.She is warm and not cold, so she must not be furry.Which she isn\'t, so that\'s confirmed. But maybe I\'m overlooking something.</think>.\n\nA) True.\n\nThe context explicitly states "Anne is cold," which directly confirms the statement. None of the provided rules or additional information contradict this fact. Therefore, the statement "Anne is cold" is **True**. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
