harlie is young (from 1).Let\'s see if any of the other premises affect that.The first conditional (8) says that if Charlie is round and quiet, then Charlie isn\'t young.But since Charlie is young, that means the antecedent (round and quiet) must be false.So either Charlie isn\'t round, or isn\'t quiet, or both.But we don\'t know yet whether Charlie is round or quiet.\n\nLooking at other rules, maybe we can find more info.Let\'s check rule 10: All blue things are young.Since Charlie is young, but we don\'t know if Charlie is blue.If Charlie were blue, then that would be consistent with rule 10, but Charlie\'s color isn\'t stated except in the question about white.\n\nRule 14 is important here: If Charlie is big and young, then Charlie isn\'t smart.Since Charlie is young, if he\'s also big, he can\'t be smart.But we don\'t know if Charlie is big or smart yet.\n\nRule 15 says round things are big.So if Charlie were round, he would have to be big.But again, we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nLooking at rule 12: Young, white things are round.So if something is both young and white, then it must be round.Since Charlie is young, if he were white, then he would have to be round.But then, if he\'s round and if he\'s also quiet (from rule 8), that would conflict with him being young.Wait, but let\'s see.\n\nSuppose Charlie is white.Then, according to rule 12, since he\'s young and white, he must be round.So Charlie would be round.Then, if he\'s round, by rule 15, he must be big.Now, if Charlie is big and young (as per rule 14), then he can\'t be smart.So Charlie would not be smart.But does that cause any contradictions?\n\nWait, but is there any information that Charlie is or isn\'t smart?Let me check.The only mention of smartness is in rule 7 (Harry is smart), rule 9 (if quiet then smart), and rule 14 (if Charlie is big and young, then not smart).So if Charlie were white, then he would be round (from 12), hence big (from 15).Then, since he\'s big and young, rule 14 would say he\'s not smart.But is there any rule that requires Charlie to be smart?Not directly.Unless there\'s another path.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is big and blue, then by rule 11 (big, blue things are quiet), he would be quiet.Then, if he\'s quiet, by rule 9, he must be smart.But if he\'s also big and young, then rule 14 would say he\'s not smart.So that would create a contradiction.Let\'s see if that path is possible.\n\nSuppose Charlie is blue.Then, since all blue things are young (rule 10), that\'s okay.If Charlie is blue and big, then by rule 11 he\'s quiet, so he must be smart (rule 9).But then, if he\'s big and young (he is young), then rule 14 says he can\'t be smart.So that would be a contradiction.Therefore, Charlie cannot be both blue and big.Therefore, if Charlie is blue, he can\'t be big.Alternatively, if Charlie is big, he can\'t be blue.But that\'s a side track for now.\n\nBack to the main question: Is Charlie not white?Let\'s see if Charlie can be white.\n\nAssume Charlie is white.Then by rule 12, he\'s round.Then, since he\'s round, he\'s big (rule 15).So Charlie is big and young, so by rule 14, he can\'t be smart.But is there a problem with that?Not yet.But let\'s see if being round and quiet would cause issues.Since he\'s round, if he is quiet, then by rule 8, since he\'s round and quiet, he can\'t be young.But he is young, so he can\'t be both round and quiet.Since he is round (from being white and young), he must not be quiet.So Charlie would not be quiet.\n\nNow, to be quiet, per rule 11, he would need to be big and blue.But if Charlie is white, he might not be blue.Wait, if Charlie is white, is he blue?The context doesn\'t say.Fiona is blue, Gary is blue and white, so maybe objects can have multiple colors.So Charlie could be white without being blue.But if he\'s white and young and round (from rule 12), but not necessarily blue.\n\nWait, but let\'s see if there\'s any rule that would require Charlie to be blue if he\'s something else.Let\'s see