tate that.The rules might have implications but not necessarily contradict Anne\'s attributes.\n\nWait, let\'s check the other rules.Rule 5 says "Cold, furry things are white."Anne is cold, furry, and white, so that holds.Rule 7: "If something is white and furry then it is smart."Anne is white, furry, and smart, so that\'s okay.\n\nRule 4: "If Fiona is rough and cold then Fiona is smart."Fiona is cold and furry, but her roughness isn\'t listed in the initial attributes.Fiona\'s attributes are cold and furry, so unless Fiona is rough, this rule wouldn\'t apply.Since Fiona\'s attributes don\'t include rough, then the antecedent is false, so the implication holds.\n\nNow, looking at the question again: is Anne rough?The context directly states "Anne is rough."So unless there\'s a rule that would invalidate that, it should be true.But maybe the rules require some condition that Anne doesn\'t meet, which would mean that her being rough is impossible?\n\nWait, let me check all rules again.Let\'s see if any rule would conflict with Anne being rough.\n\nRule 6: "All rough, nice things are young."But Anne isn\'t nice, so that doesn\'t apply to her.\n\nRule 2: "If Bob is young and cold, then Bob is furry."Since Bob is rough and nice, according to rule 6 he must be young, so Bob is young.Then, since Bob is young and cold (because young things are cold per rule 3), then he must be furry.But Bob\'s attributes don\'t list him as furry.Wait a second, that\'s a problem.Because according to the initial context, Bob is not listed as furry, but the rules would require him to be furry if he\'s young and cold.Since he must be young (because he\'s rough and nice), and young things are cold, so Bob must be cold and young, so he must be furry.But the initial listing says Bob is not furry.That creates a contradiction.\n\nHmm, this might mean that the initial listing of Bob\'s attributes is incomplete, and we have to take the rules into account.Therefore, Bob must be furry even if not listed