martness.\n\nHmm.Maybe there\'s a missing link here.Let me see if there\'s another angle.\n\nWait, the problem says "All quiet, nice things are furry."(rule13).But if something is quiet and nice, then it\'s furry.But quiet already implies furry from rule12.So maybe this is just reinforcing, but not adding new info.\n\nAlternatively, maybe if something is both quiet and nice, then it\'s furry.But since quiet → furry is already a rule, this might not add anything.So maybe redundant.\n\nBack to Bob.Since he\'s green and kind, can we get to smart via another path?\n\nWait, let\'s see if being kind implies anything else besides green.The rules don\'t state that.Unless via another route.For instance, if being kind and young would imply something else?\n\nAlternatively, maybe if someone is kind and young, then... Not sure.Let\'s see.\n\nWait, Bob is kind.Suppose he is young.Then from rule11, that\'s okay.But how do we know if he\'s young?\n\nAlternatively, maybe Fiona\'s attributes can help.Fiona is young and nice, so she\'s also kind (from rule11).So Fiona is kind, nice, and young.Since she\'s young and nice, and if she were green, then she\'d be smart via rule14.But since we don\'t know if she\'s green, that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Charlie\'s info.Charlie is green (fact3) and not smart (fact4).So Charlie is green but not smart.So he can\'t be quiet because he\'s not smart.So that\'s consistent.\n\nErin is not young (fact5).So Erin\'s age is not young, so by rule10, if she were smart, she would have to be young.Since she\'s not young, she can\'t be smart.But that\'s an inference.\n\nHmm.So perhaps Bob\'s smartness is unknown.Because there\'s no rule that makes him smart unless we can find another path.\n\nWait, let\'s check rule14 again: "Nice, green things are smart."So if something is both nice and green, then it\'s smart.But Bob isn\'t nice, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nAnother thought: Since Bob is green and kind, and kind things are green (so that\'s oka