e.The rabbit visits the cat (statement 10).Then by statement 16, that means the rabbit must visit the rabbit (since visiting the cat implies visiting the rabbit).But that\'s a bit odd, but maybe the rabbit does visit itself.However, that\'s not really useful for determining size.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain here.Since the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), by statement 16, the rabbit must visit the rabbit.But that\'s a bit of a loop.Let\'s see if that leads to anything else.Suppose the rabbit visits the rabbit.Then, perhaps applying other rules.For example, if something visits the rabbit and sees the cow, then it must visit the cow (statement 17).The rabbit does see the cow (statement 9).So if the rabbit visits the rabbit and sees the cow, then it must visit the cow.Wait, but visiting the rabbit would be the rabbit visiting itself.Let\'s see:\n\nIf the rabbit (X) visits the rabbit (itself) and sees the cow (which it does), then according to statement 17, X (the rabbit) must visit the cow.So that would mean the rabbit visits the cow.But the original statements don\'t say that the rabbit visits the cow, but according to this implication, if the rabbit does visit itself (due to visiting the cat leading to visiting the rabbit), then it would have to visit the cow.However, we can\'t confirm whether the rabbit actually visits itself.Wait, the chain is: visiting the cat implies visiting the rabbit (statement 16).Since the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), then the rabbit must visit the rabbit (itself).So that\'s a necessary conclusion.Therefore, the rabbit does visit itself.Then, since it visits the rabbit (itself) and sees the cow, by statement 17, it must visit the cow.Therefore, the rabbit must visit the cow.\n\nBut does the rabbit visiting the cow lead to anything?Let\'s see.If the rabbit visits the cow, then we can check other implications.For example, statement 11: If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle.The 