cow, so it must visit the cow.So the rabbit must visit the cow.\n\nStep 3: Now, the rabbit visits the cow.Then, since it also sees the cow (statement 9), the antecedent of statement 11 is met: "If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle."So the rabbit must visit the bald eagle.\n\nStep 4: The rabbit now visits the bald eagle.Therefore, by statement 13: "If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice."So the rabbit is nice.\n\nStep 5: Then, by statement 12: "If something is nice, then it is big."Therefore, the rabbit is big.So that would make the answer A) True?\n\nBut wait, is there any contradiction here?Let\'s check if all the steps are valid and there\'s no missing information that could invalidate this.\n\nWait, but in the original premises, the rabbit is already stated to eat the bald eagle (statement 7).Eating and visiting are different actions, but they don\'t conflict.The rabbit can both eat and visit the bald eagle.So visiting the bald eagle would be an additional action that the rabbit must do based on the implications.Since the problem allows for multiple actions, that\'s okay.\n\nHowever, the problem is whether all these implications are correctly applied.Let me verify again:\n\nStarting with the rabbit\'s visit to the cat (statement 10) leading to visiting itself via statement 16.Then that visit plus seeing the cow leads to visiting the cow (statement 17).Then visiting and seeing the cow leads to visiting the bald eagle (statement 11).Then visiting the bald eagle makes the rabbit nice (statement 13), hence big (statement 12).So yes, that seems to hold.\n\nBut wait, the problem is that in the initial statements, there\'s no mention of the rabbit visiting the bald eagle.But according to the logic, it must.So unless there\'s a rule that prevents that, it should hold.Since all the premises are given as true, the logical steps must be followed.Therefore, the conclusion is that the rabbit is big.So the answer would be 