ts the squirrel and the squirrel is big, then it does not like the squirrel.\n15.If something is big, then it visits the cow.\n16.If something visits the squirrel and it is not red, then the squirrel does not visit the bald eagle.\n\nThe question is asking whether "The cow is not red" is true, false, or unknown.Wait, but looking at statement 5, it says "The cow is red."So that directly states the cow\'s color.If that\'s the case, then the statement "The cow is not red" would be false, right?Because the cow is red according to the given information.But maybe there\'s a contradiction somewhere else that I\'m missing?\n\nLet me double-check.The problem might be a trick question where even though statement 5 says the cow is red, some other rule might imply otherwise.Let me go through the other statements to see if there\'s any conflicting info.\n\nStarting with the bald eagle: it\'s big, cold, and young.From statement 9, all big things are cold, which aligns with the bald eagle being big and cold.So that checks out.\n\nStatement 6: the cow likes the bald eagle.Then statement 8 says if something likes the cow, then the cow likes the squirrel.Wait, but here the cow is liking the bald eagle, not the other way around.So maybe that\'s not directly relevant yet.\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 7).Then statement 16 says if something visits the squirrel and it is not red, then the squirrel does not visit the bald eagle.Wait, but the squirrel is visiting the bald eagle, so maybe that would imply that if the squirrel were not red, then the squirrel wouldn\'t be visiting the bald eagle, but since it is visiting, that would require that the visitor (if any) to the squirrel must be red?Hmm, maybe I need to parse that more carefully.\n\nStatement 16: If something visits the squirrel and it is not red, then the squirrel does not visit the bald eagle.The antecedent here is "something visits the squirrel AND the something is not red".The consequent is "the squirrel does