tes "the mouse eats the tiger", but eating might require seeing, but it\'s not stated.The problem says "the mouse does not see the tiger" (statement9), so the mouse can\'t see the tiger even though it eats it.So that\'s possible.\n\nWait, statement9 says "The mouse does not see the tiger."So the mouse can\'t see the tiger, but it can eat it.So seeing and eating are separate actions.\n\nNow, if the tiger is big (which we don\'t know yet), then perhaps via another rule.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat sees the dog.Wait, the dog sees the cat (statement6), but the cat might not see the dog.\n\nHmm.Let me consider all possibilities again.Since the question is whether the tiger sees the dog, and there\'s no direct statement that says it does, we have to see if any of the conditional statements can lead us there.\n\nLooking at statement19 again.If the tiger is both green and big, then it sees the dog.Since we don\'t know if the tiger is green or big, this is a conditional that might or might not apply.\n\nIs there any way to determine if the tiger is big?Let\'s see.If the tiger sees the mouse, then by statement14 it must be big.Does the tiger see the mouse?The mouse eats the tiger (statement8), but the mouse doesn\'t see the tiger (statement9).The tiger might see the mouse, but there\'s no direct statement.\n\nAlternatively, if the tiger chases the mouse, does that imply anything?The cat chases the mouse (statement2) and the tiger isn\'t mentioned chasing the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is big because of another reason.The dog is big (statement5), but that doesn\'t affect the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is considered big by default?The problem doesn\'t state that.\n\nSince we can\'t confirm that the tiger is green or big, the condition in statement19 isn\'t met, so we can\'t use it to conclude the tiger sees the dog.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if any other conditional rules could lead us to the tiger seeing the dog.\n\nLooking at statement18: If