gs are furry.So red → furry.\n\nPremise 9: All furry things are red.So furry → red.Therefore, red ↔ furry.So anything red is furry and vice versa.\n\nPremise 12: All furry things are green.Since furry → red (from 9), then furry things are also green.So furry → green.Therefore, since red ↔ furry, then red → green as well.So all red things are green.Hmm.\n\nPremise 10: Young things are red.So Charlie, being young, is red.Therefore, Charlie is red → furry (from 8), and since furry → green (12), Charlie must be green.Wait, but the question is asking if Charlie is not green.If that\'s the case, then the answer would be False.But wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake here.Let me go through each step carefully.\n\nStarting with Charlie:\n\n- Charlie is young (premise 1).Therefore, by premise 10 (young → red), Charlie is red.\n\n- Since Charlie is red, by premise 8 (red → furry), Charlie is furry.\n\n- By premise 12 (furry → green), Charlie must be green.\n\nTherefore, Charlie is green.The question is asking if "Charlie is not green" is true.Since we just concluded Charlie is green, then the statement "Charlie is not green" would be false.So the answer should be B) False?\n\nWait, but let me check if there are any contradictions or other rules that might affect this.\n\nWait, also, looking at premise 11: If something is smart and not red, then it\'s not green.But since Charlie is red, this doesn\'t apply to him.So that\'s okay.\n\nAdditionally, premise 12 says all furry things are green.Since Charlie is furry (from being red), he must be green.So that seems solid.\n\nWait, but let\'s check other premises.Let\'s see if there\'s any conflicting information.\n\nPremise 4 says Fiona is big and green.Fiona\'s other attributes?Not mentioned.Fiona is big, so if being big has any implications?The other premises related to big are premise 10 (young → red), and the rules about smartness.\n\nPremise 10: Young → red.So Charlie is red, so he\'s green (from above).\n\nNow, let\'s look at other rules that might affect Charlie:\n\nIs there any rule that could make Charlie not green?Let\'s see.\n\nAnother premise is premise 13 (if I count correctly, the numbering might be off, but let me recount):\n\nWait, the premises given in the context are numbered 1 to 12?Let me check again:\n\nThe problem lists:\n\n1.Charlie is young.\n\n2.Erin is blue.\n\n3.Erin is green.\n\n4.Fiona is big.\n\n5.Fiona is green.\n\n6.Gary is blue.\n\n7.Gary is green.\n\n8.All red things are furry.\n\n9.All furry things are red.\n\n10.Young things are red.\n\n11.If something is smart and not red then it is not green.\n\n12.All furry things are green.\n\n13.If something is red and green then it is blue.\n\nWait, the user listed the premises up to "If something is blue and green then it is smart.Blue, red things are smart."Wait, maybe I missed some.\n\nWait, let me recount the premises as presented:\n\nThe user wrote:\n\n"Context:\n\nCharlie is young.Erin is blue.Erin is green.Fiona is big.Fiona is green.Gary is blue.Gary is green.All red things are furry.All furry things are red.Young things are red.If something is smart and not red then it is not green.All furry things are green.If something is red and green then it is blue.If something is young and big then it is smart.If something is blue and green then it is smart.Blue, red things are smart."\n\nWait, so after "All furry things are green" comes:\n\n"If something is red and green then it is blue."(That\'s premise 12?Or 13?)\n\nWait, let me parse all the premises step by step as listed in the problem\'s context:\n\nLet me list them in order as presented:\n\n1.Charlie is young.\n\n2.Erin is blue.\n\n3.Erin is green.\n\n4.Fiona is big.\n\n5.Fiona is green.\n\n6.Gary is blue.\n\n7.Gary is green.\n\n8.All red things are furry.\n\n9.All furry things are red.\n\n10.Young things are red.\n\n11.If something is smart and not red then it is not green.\n\n12.All furry things are green.\n\n13.If something is