nce, does the rabbit's liking affect anything? The rabbit likes the dog (given), but that's separate.\n\nAlternatively, is there a condition that says if someone likes the rabbit, they must be rough? Let me check the seventh conditional:\n\n\"If someone is rough and they like the rabbit then the rabbit is young.\"\n\nThe cat is rough (given), and if the cat likes the rabbit, then the rabbit must be young. But is the rabbit young?\n\nThe rabbit's age isn't stated directly except in the question's context. The cat is young (statement 3), but the rabbit's age is unknown unless inferred.\n\nIf the rabbit must be young because of this, does that hold?\n\nIf the cat likes the rabbit (from above), and the cat is rough (given), then according to the seventh conditional, the rabbit must be young. So the rabbit is young.\n\nIs there any condition that would contradict the rabbit being young? Let me see.\n\nThe eighth conditional: \"If the rabbit chases the cat and the cat needs the lion then the rabbit is young.\"\n\nBut does the rabbit chase the cat? The rabbit chases the lion (statement 11), but not necessarily the cat. So unless the rabbit chases the cat, this doesn't apply. Since we don't have that info, the rabbit's youth via this conditional isn't triggered unless we have that.\n\nHowever, through the seventh conditional, if the cat likes the rabbit and is rough, then rabbit is young. So that gives us rabbit is young. Since the cat does like the rabbit (from previous), then rabbit must be young. That's okay.\n\nBut the rabbit's age wasn't given, but this is a valid inference.\n\nAlternatively, could there be a contradiction? Suppose the rabbit isn't young, but that would violate the seventh conditional. So the rabbit must be young.\n\nTherefore, everything checks out. The conclusion holds.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) False, because the statement is false.\n</think>\n\nThe cat chases the lion, making it round (first conditional). As a round lion-chaser, the cat must n