t eats mouse) is false, then the implication (statement 15) is false, but the problem states that implication as a given.\n\nTherefore, that can\'t be the case.Therefore, the only way for the implication to hold is if the consequent is true (cat eats mouse) even if that requires the cat to be round, even though it\'s not stated.\n\nWait, but the problem says "if something eats the mouse then it is round", but it doesn\'t say that only round things can eat the mouse.It\'s a one-way implication.So, if the cat eats the mouse, then it must be round.But the problem doesn\'t say that if something is not round, it can\'t eat the mouse.\n\nHowever, the problem doesn\'t state the cat is round, so the cat could potentially be round or not.But if the cat eats the mouse, it must be round.If the cat is not round, then it can\'t eat the mouse.\n\nBut from the previous steps, the cat must eat the mouse (due to the mouse chasing the cat and statement 15), so that would require the cat to be round.Since the problem doesn\'t state that the cat is round, but allows it to be, perhaps we can infer that the cat must be round to satisfy the condition.\n\nWait, but the problem only gives us the premises listed.The cat\'s roundness isn\'t mentioned, so we can\'t assume it\'s round unless required.\n\nHmm, this is getting a bit tangled.Let me try to approach this with formal logic.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s define:\n\n- Let C be the cat, M the mouse, B the bald eagle, and W the cow.\n\nGiven:\n\n1.B chases C. (B → chase C)\n2.B doesn’t eat M. (¬(B → eat M))\n3.C eats W. (C → eat W)\n4.W eats C. (W → eat C)\n5.W is blue.(W → blue)The cat does not eat the mouse.</think>.\n\nTo determine if "The cat eats the mouse" is true, we analyze the given statements step by step:.\n\n1. **Mouse is round (statement 11)**. By statement 16 ("If something is round, it chases the cat"), the mouse must chase the cat.\n2. **Mouse chases the cat** (from steps 1 and 16). By statement 15 ("If the mouse chases the 