round, it must be young. Harry is young and nice, so if he were round, that would satisfy the rule. But since he is young and nice, but not necessarily round, there's no contradiction. So he can be either.\n\nAlternatively, could there be a rule that Harry must be round because of his youth and niceness? For example, is there a rule that says young and nice things must be round? No, rule 8 is the other way: nice and round \u2192 young. It doesn't impose that being young and nice requires roundness.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but wait another thought: Let me see if there's a way to force Harry to be round.\n\nSuppose Harry is not round. Then he can't be rough (due to rule 3). But could there be another path to roughness?\n\nThe only other rule that gives roughness is rule 5: round and nice \u2192 rough. So if he's not round, that path is closed. The other is rule 2: cold and furry \u2192 rough. But he can't be cold unless he's round (from rule 1). Since he's not round, he can't be cold. So no, there's no other way to get roughness if not round. Thus, not round implies not rough.\n\nTherefore, the two possibilities are:\n\nRound \u2192 rough \u2192 statement \"Harry is not rough\" is false.\n\nNot round \u2192 not rough \u2192 statement is true.\n\nSince we can't determine which is the case, the truth value is unknown. So the answer is C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check the problem's context again to see if I missed any rules.\n\nWait, the context says \"All round, nice things are rough.\" (rule 5). So if Harry is round and nice, he must be rough. But he is nice already. So if he's round, he must be rough. If he's not round, he can avoid that.\n\nHowever, another angle: Is there a way Harry must be round? Let me see.\n\nHarry is young. The only rule involving youth besides the given attributes is rule 8: nice and round things are young. But that doesn't require youth to be caused by roundness. So Harry's youth is already