tiger.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bald eagle is cold.If it\'s cold, then by statement 11, it would see the rabbit.But does seeing the rabbit lead to anything?Well, if the bald eagle sees the rabbit, then by statement 14, the rabbit eats the bald eagle.But the rabbit already eats the tiger (statement 6).The rabbit eating the bald eagle would make the rabbit cold (statement 15), but that\'s about the rabbit\'s temperature, not the bald eagle\'s.\n\nAlternatively, if the bald eagle is cold, then seeing the rabbit (from statement 11) would lead to the rabbit eating the bald eagle (via statement 14).But that creates a loop: the bald eagle is cold → sees rabbit → rabbit eats bald eagle → which via statement 15 would make the rabbit cold (since the rabbit is eating the bald eagle).Wait, no: statement 15 says if someone eats the bald eagle, they are cold.So the rabbit would be cold if it eats the bald eagle.But the rabbit is the eater, so the rabbit would be cold, not the bald eagle.So the bald eagle\'s coldness is a separate condition.\n\nBut how does the bald eagle get cold?Unless it eats something, but the bald eagle only eats the tiger (statement 1).There\'s no condition that says eating the tiger makes it cold.So the bald eagle\'s coldness is unknown unless another condition applies.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle eats the tiger, and that somehow leads to seeing the tiger?Not sure.\n\nWait, another angle: Let\'s suppose that the bald eagle does not see the tiger.Then, the condition in statement 12 isn\'t triggered, so we can\'t conclude anything from that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to chain conditions.Let\'s see:\n\nSuppose the bald eagle is cold.Then by statement 11, it sees the rabbit.Then by statement 14, the rabbit eats the bald eagle.Now, the rabbit eating the bald eagle would mean that the rabbit is cold (from statement 15).But the rabbit\'s coldness is irrelevant to the bald eagle\'s seeing.\n\nAlternatively, if the bald eagle sees the ra