2, so Harry must be red.\n\nBut how does that help with Charlie?\n\nMaybe not directly, but just an example of how to apply the rules.\n\nBack to Charlie.Since Charlie is white, maybe there\'s a rule that connects being white to something else.Let me see:\n\nRule14: Blue and red people are white.So if someone is both blue and red, they are white.But that\'s about being blue and red leading to white, not the other way around.\n\nRule13: White and rough implies kind.But again, that\'s a forward direction.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that all the rules don\'t mention Charlie having any other attributes besides white, so unless there\'s a rule that says all white people must be rough or something, but I don\'t see that.The only rules about roughness are:\n\n- Rule15: Nice people are rough.So if someone is nice, they are rough.But Charlie isn\'t mentioned as nice.\n\n- Rule13: White and rough people are kind.So if someone is both, they are kind.\n\n- Rule16: If someone is kind and furry, then they are rough.So if someone is both kind and furry, then they must be rough.But again, Charlie\'s attributes?\n\nWait, maybe Charlie could be kind.Suppose Charlie is kind.Then, by rule8 (kind and white implies furry), he would be furry.But we don\'t know if he\'s furry.Alternatively, if he is kind and furry, then by rule16, he must be rough.But again, without knowing if he\'s kind or furry, that\'s not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, since Charlie is white, is there any rule that requires him to have another attribute?Not that I can see.\n\nWait, let me think again.The question is whether Charlie is rough.We know he\'s white.Are there any paths that could lead to him being rough or not?\n\nSince he\'s not nice (no info on that, but since he\'s not mentioned as nice, we can\'t assume), so rule15 doesn\'t apply.Unless there\'s another way to be rough.The only other rule that mentions roughness is rule13 and rule16.\n\nRule13 is about white and rough implying kind.So if he were rough,