icting.Hmm, maybe that\'s a typo or maybe there\'s a rule I\'m missing.Let me check again.The problem says "the bear is blue", then "the bear is green", then "the bear is red".That seems contradictory because an object can\'t be three different colors at once.Maybe I need to consider that these are separate statements, but maybe there\'s a condition attached?Or perhaps they are part of different premises?Wait, maybe the problem is structured so that each line is a separate fact, but then the bear can\'t have multiple colors.That might be an error.Or maybe the colors are changing?Hmm, that complicates things.Maybe I should note that the bear\'s color is conflicting and see if the rules can resolve that somehow.\n\nNext statements:\n\n- The bear sees the rabbit.\n- The cow is blue.\n- The cow needs the tiger.\n- The cow visits the rabbit.\n- The rabbit is cold.\n- The rabbit is nice.\n- The rabbit needs the cow.\n- The rabbit sees the cow.\n- The rabbit sees the tiger.\n- The tiger needs the bear.\n- The tiger needs the rabbit.\n- The tiger visits the bear.\n- The tiger visits the cow.\n\nThen there are a bunch of conditional statements.Let me list those out:\n\n1.If something is green then it visits the tiger.(G → VT)\n2.If something sees the bear and it is cold then the bear is green.(SB ∧ C → B is G)\n3.If the cow needs the rabbit and the rabbit needs the cow then the rabbit is red.(CNR ∧ RNC → R is R)\n4.If something is green then it needs the cow.(G → NC)\n5.If something visits the tiger then the tiger is red.(VT → T is R)\n6.If something needs the tiger and the tiger sees the cow then the cow needs the bear.(NT ∧ TSC → CNB)\n7.If something sees the bear and it needs the bear then it sees the cow.(SB ∧ NB → SC)\n8.If something visits the rabbit then the rabbit needs the tiger.(VR → RNT)\n9.If something needs the bear and the bear visits the tiger then it sees the bear.(NB ∧ BVT → SB)\n\nWait, I need to parse the conditionals correctly.Let me rephrase each one wit