sn't big.\n\nTherefore, the only fact we need is Fiona is rough, so the statement Fiona is not rough is false. So the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but the user's first draft had a confusion where they thought the answer was A (True) but realized it's B. But the correct answer is B. So the user should select option B.\n\nBut let me check again if there's any rule that might make Fiona not rough.\n\nLooking at the conditional statements:\n\nThe problem says \"If someone is white and smart then they are furry.\" But Fiona's attributes don't include being white or smart unless inferred.\n\nWait, Fiona is rough, so she must be big (premise 9). Then, premise 15: If someone is big, then they are smart. So Fiona is big (from being rough), so she must be smart. So Fiona is smart. Then, since Fiona is big and smart, premise 14 says big, smart people are quiet. So Fiona must be quiet. But the context says Fiona is cold. Wait, premise 11 says if someone is quiet and cold, then they are white. So Fiona would be quiet (from big and smart), cold (given), so quiet and cold implies she must be white. Then, being white and smart (since she is smart), then from premise 8, being white and smart would require her to be furry. \n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nFiona is rough \u2192 Fiona is big (premise 9).\n\nFiona is big \u2192 Fiona is smart (premise 15).\n\nFiona is big and smart \u2192 Fiona is quiet (premise 14).\n\nFiona is quiet and cold (since she's quiet from above and cold from premise 2) \u2192 Fiona is white (premise 11).\n\nFiona is white and smart \u2192 Fiona is furry (premise 8).\n\nAll these are inferences, but none of them contradict her being rough. So Fiona is rough, and also big, smart, quiet, cold, white, and furry. But there's no contradiction here. Therefore, the original statement that Fiona is rough is still valid, so the question's statement \"Fiona is not rough\" is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe context explici