s white then it is nice."Fiona is indeed nice, so that holds, but that doesn\'t add new info.\n\nAlso, Fiona is nice, so by statement 12, if something is nice then it\'s round.Therefore Fiona must be round.So Fiona is round.\n\nThen, since Fiona is round, by statement 16, round things are blue.Therefore Fiona must be blue.\n\nWait, so Fiona is blue?Because she\'s round (from being nice) and round implies blue.\n\nNow, since Fiona is blue, we can apply other rules.From statement 8: All blue things are white.That\'s consistent with Fiona already being white, so that\'s okay.\n\nAlso, statement 14 says all blue things are young.Fiona is already young, so that\'s okay too.\n\nNow, looking at statement 10: All young, blue things are not green.Since Fiona is young and blue, she cannot be green.\n\nBut the question is about Fiona being cold.Let\'s see if there\'s any connection between Fiona\'s attributes and being cold.\n\nLooking at the premises, the cold things mentioned are Charlie, Dave.Fiona\'s attributes so far are nice, white, young, round, blue.\n\nIs there a rule that connects coldness with any of Fiona\'s attributes?Let\'s check the rules again.\n\nRule 15: If something is cold and green then it is young.But Fiona is not green (since she\'s blue and young, so by rule 10, she can\'t be green).So that might not apply here.\n\nAnother angle: The only things stated to be cold are Charlie and Dave.There\'s no direct statement about Fiona\'s temperature.However, maybe there\'s a rule that could imply Fiona\'s temperature?\n\nWait, let\'s see if Fiona\'s being blue and young (since blue things are young) has any implication on being cold.The rules about coldness are only in statements 1 (Charlie is cold), 3 (Dave is cold), and 15.\n\nRule 15: If something is cold and green then it is young.Since Fiona isn\'t green, that doesn\'t affect her.\n\nIs there any rule that links being blue, nice, round, etc., to being cold or not?Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 13: If Charlie is round then Charlie is white.But Charlie\'s status as round isn\'t given directly, but Charlie is young and cold.Not sure if that\'s relevant here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction if Fiona were cold, which would make the statement "Fiona is not cold" necessarily true.Let\'s check that.\n\nSuppose Fiona is cold.Let\'s see if that would violate any rules.\n\nIf Fiona is cold, then since she is blue (from earlier deductions), and blue is a color.But is there any rule that says blue things can\'t be cold?Not that I see.The coldness is given for Charlie and Dave, but no general rule against others being cold.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if being cold and blue would lead to something.Fiona is blue, so she\'s young (from 14).If she were cold and green, then by 15, she would have to be young, which she is, but she\'s not green.So that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, are there any rules that could require Fiona to be cold?Not that I can see.The premises state Charlie and Dave are cold, but Fiona\'s attributes don\'t lead to any conclusion about her being cold or not.\n\nTherefore, the information given doesn\'t state anything about Fiona\'s temperature except what can be inferred.Since there\'s no rule that connects her attributes (being blue, round, etc.)to being cold or not, and the premises don\'t mention her being cold, we can\'t definitively say she is cold.Therefore, the statement "Fiona is not cold" would be true unless there\'s some hidden implication.\n\nWait a second, perhaps there\'s a rule I missed.Let me check again.\n\nLooking at all the statements again:\n\nRule 16: Round things are blue.Fiona is round, so blue.\n\nRule 14: All blue are young.Fiona is young.\n\nRule 10: Young and blue things are not green.Fiona is not green.\n\nRule 11: White things are nice.Fiona is nice.\n\nRule 12: Nice things are round.Fiona is round.\n\nSo all of Fiona\'s attributes are covered by these.\n\nIs there any rule that connects being nice, round, blue, etc., to