).However, maybe there\'s a contradiction or some other rule that might affect this?\n\nWait, let me check all the other statements to ensure there\'s no conflict.Let me go step by step.\n\nFirst, looking at Harry\'s attributes:\n\n- Harry is big (4)\n- Harry is not green (5)\n- Harry is quiet (6)\n- Harry is red (7)\n\nSo according to the given statements, Harry is explicitly stated to be red.Therefore, the statement "Harry is not red" would be false, so the answer should be B) False.But maybe there\'s some hidden contradiction?\n\nWait, let\'s check the other rules.Let\'s see if any of the conditional statements might affect this.\n\nFirst, rule 8: Quiet, red people are smart.Since Harry is both quiet (6) and red (7), that would make Harry smart.\n\nThen rule 9: If someone is quiet, they are smart.Since Harry is quiet, that also makes him smart.So both rules 8 and 9 confirm that Harry is smart.\n\nRule 15 says all smart people are red.Since Harry is smart, that would require him to be red, which aligns with point 7.So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, let\'s check if there\'s any rule that might say Harry can\'t be red.Let\'s see.\n\nRule 5: Harry is not green.So that\'s okay, since being red doesn\'t conflict with not being green.\n\nRule 13: If someone is white, then they are quiet.Fiona is white (3), so she must be quiet.But that\'s okay, doesn\'t affect Harry.\n\nRule 14: Rough people are green.Fiona is white (3), and if she were both white and red (from rule 12), she would be rough.But Fiona is white, but is she red?The problem says Fiona is white, but there\'s no mention of her being red except in rule 12\'s conditional.Since Fiona is white, unless she\'s also red, then rule 12 doesn\'t apply.Since there\'s no statement that Fiona is red, Fiona is only white, so rule 12 doesn\'t trigger, so Fiona isn\'t rough, so she\'s not green (since rough people are green, but she\'s not rough).So that\'s okay, no conflict here.\n\nLooking at Dave\'s attributes:\n\nDave is red