own.\n\nScenario 3: Gary is not rough but is nice \u2192 then he must be white (premise 11). So in this case, he is white.\n\nWait, if Gary is nice (regardless of being rough or not), he must be white. But does Gary have to be nice?\n\nWe don't know if Gary is nice or not. The premises only state Gary's coldness and quietness. So:\n\n- If Gary is nice (whether rough or not), then he is white.\n- If Gary is not nice, then his color is unknown (could be white or not).\n\nThus, there are two possibilities:\n\nEither:\n\n1. Gary is nice \u2192 white (so \"not white\" is false).\n\nOR\n\n2. Gary is not nice \u2192 could be white or not (so \"not white\" could be true or false).\n\nSince we don't know whether Gary is nice or not, we can't determine his whiteness. Thus, the statement \"Gary is not white\" could be false (if he is nice/white) or true (if not nice and not white) or even false in the not-nice case if he is white but not nice (since premise 11 only requires nice things to be white, but doesn't say white things must be nice). Wait, that's a key point.\n\nPremise 11: \"Nice things are white.\" This is an implication: if nice \u2192 white. The converse isn't stated. So something can be white without being nice. Therefore, even if Gary isn't nice, he could still be white, or not. Since there's no rule about non-nice things' color, we can't say.\n\nTherefore, even if Gary isn't nice, he could be white or not. Thus, the statement \"Gary is not white\" could be true or false regardless of his niceness. Hence, it's unknown.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Unknown. The earlier conclusion was correct. The assistant's initial thought process had a detailed analysis leading to unknown, so the final answer is C.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{C}",
