ement 8) means the mouse is the eater, and the rabbit is the eaten.So the eater is the mouse, so the condition here would be if something (the mouse) eats the rabbit, then the mouse needs the cat.Wait, but statement 14 says "if something eats the mouse..." so the condition is when an entity eats the mouse, then the mouse needs the cat.\n\nWait, let me parse that again.Statement 14: "If something eats the mouse then the mouse needs the cat."So the structure is: If X eats the mouse, then the mouse needs the cat.\n\nSo, for example, if the lion eats the mouse, then the mouse must need the cat.But in our case, the mouse eats the rabbit (statement 8), so the mouse isn\'t being eaten by anyone here yet.Unless there\'s another entity eating the mouse.The problem doesn\'t mention that.So unless there\'s someone else eating the mouse, this condition might not apply.\n\nMoving on to statement 16: "If something chases the rabbit then the rabbit is blue."\n\nWe already established that the lion is blue and therefore chases the rabbit (from 15).Therefore, by 16, the rabbit must be blue.Wait, but the original statements didn\'t say the rabbit\'s color.So according to this, since the lion (which is blue) is chasing the rabbit, then the rabbit must be blue.\n\nWait, but statement 16 says "If something chases the rabbit, then the rabbit is blue."So if the lion is chasing the rabbit, then the rabbit is blue.So that would set the rabbit\'s color to blue.\n\nBut none of the original statements mention the rabbit\'s color.So that\'s an implication from these rules.So the rabbit must be blue.\n\nNow, let\'s check if there are any contradictions here.\n\nThe lion is blue, so it chases the rabbit (statement 15), so rabbit must be blue (statement 16).That\'s okay.\n\nNow, what about the rabbit needing the cat?\n\nWe need to see if there\'s a rule that would require the rabbit to need the cat, or not.\n\nLooking at statement 17: "If something needs the cat and it is not big then it is kind."