ts the squirrel...", so if X is the someone who visits BE, then BE chases the squirrel.Since BE does visit the squirrel, then if X visits BE, then BE chases S. But does BE chase S?The squirrel chases BE (premise8), but BE chasing S isn\'t stated.Let me note that as a possible implication.\n\nBut maybe let\'s look elsewhere first.\n\nPremise 14: If someone visits the tiger and they visit the lion, then the lion needs the bald eagle.\n\nWe know that the squirrel visits the tiger (premise10).So S visits T. Does S visit the lion?Not stated yet.If S did visit the lion, then premise14 would trigger, leading to lion needing BE.But since L already needs T (premise7), but needing BE would be an additional fact.Not sure yet.\n\nPremise16: If someone is red, they chase the lion.The tiger is red (premise11).Therefore, the tiger (T) must chase the lion (L).So T chases L.\n\nSo from T being red, we can conclude T chases L (from premise16).\n\nNow, let\'s see what that leads to.If T chases L, then look at premise19: If someone chases the lion, then they visit the bald eagle.Since T chases L, then T must visit the bald eagle (BE).So T visits BE?\n\nWait, that would mean the tiger visits the bald eagle, so the statement "The tiger does not visit the bald eagle" would be false, so the answer would be B) False.But let me check again.\n\nWait, let me go step by step:\n\nPremise16: T is red, so T chases L (from premise16: If X is red → X chases L).Therefore, T chases L.\n\nThen, by premise19: If someone chases the lion, then they visit the bald eagle.Since T chases L, then T must visit BE.Therefore, T visits BE.Therefore, the statement "the tiger does not visit the bald eagle" is false.So the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut hold on, let me make sure there\'s no contradiction here.Let me check if there are any other premises that might affect this.\n\nWait, also, premise17: If someone chases the lion and they visit the bald eagle, then they need the bald eagle.Since T chases L and (if T