art.Since Gary is quiet (if our conclusion holds), then he must be smart.However, the problem doesn\'t state any restrictions on who can be smart.So unless there\'s a contradiction here, it\'s okay.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a problem here.Let me see: Since Gary is blue and young (as per premise 10), and if he\'s big and blue, then he\'s quiet.But being quiet requires being smart.Is there any reason Gary can\'t be smart?The premises don\'t say that only certain things can be smart.So unless there\'s a rule that says something else, like maybe being white or another attribute restricts smartness, but I don\'t see that.The only mention of smartness is Harry being smart, and the implication that quiet things are smart.So if Gary is quiet, he must be smart, but there\'s no rule that says he can\'t be smart.Therefore, that doesn\'t create a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, Gary is big (because round), blue, so big and blue implies quiet.Therefore, the answer should be A) True.Wait, but hold on.Let me check the premises again for any possible contradictions or missed details.\n\nWait, another thought: The problem states "All blue things are young."Since Gary is blue, he must be young, which we have.But premise 12 says young and white things are round.Gary is young and white, so he\'s round, which is big.So that\'s all consistent.\n\nPremise 11: Big and blue implies quiet.Gary is both, so quiet.Therefore, unless there\'s a condition that says something else about quietness for Gary, this holds.The only other quiet-related premise is the ninth one (quiet implies smart).Since there\'s no rule against Gary being smart, this is okay.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s an implicit assumption that only certain things can be smart.Let me check the premises again.The only explicit mentions of smartness are Harry (explicitly smart) and the rule that quiet things must be smart.So if Gary is quiet, he must be smart, but there\'s no rule that says only quiet things are