the answer must be unknown. Therefore, the correct option is C.\n\nWait but the initial problem might have a trick. Let me see the original facts again.\n\nWait, the mouse likes the tiger (fact9). So by conditional3, the mouse must visit the mouse? Which is a bit odd, but perhaps the problem allows that, but that doesn't help the bear.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bear cannot visit the mouse because the tiger eats the mouse (fact10), and if the bear visited the mouse, that would interfere? Not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's an implicit assumption that an animal can't visit another it eats, but the problem doesn't state that. The bear eats the bald eagle (fact6), but that doesn't prevent the bald eagle from visiting the bear (fact5). So eating and visiting are separate.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C) Unknown.\n\nBut wait, the user initially thought B) False? Or did I get confused?\n\nWait, in my first analysis, I thought that there was no information, so it's unknown. But let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps the bear cannot visit the mouse because of some other reason.\n\nWait, the bear is blue. Is that related to any conditional? Conditional6 says cold and kind things are big. Blue is a color, so not related.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear's color implies something else, but no.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through the mouse's big status. If the mouse is big, then... but that doesn't relate to the bear visiting.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bear's visit to the mouse would require the bear to be kind (via conditional20), and if the bear is kind and cold, then it would be big. But we don't know the bear's coldness.\n\nWait, the bear's temperature isn't mentioned. The bald eagle is cold (fact2), but the bear's temperature isn't stated. So the bear could be cold or not, so we can't assume.\n\nTherefore, the bear could visit the mouse (being kind or not, but if it does visit, then it must be kind). But there's no contradiction in that