e cow now chases the squirrel (fact 16).But that was due to the bear eating the cow.Wait, actually, the bear eating the cow (fact 15) caused the cow to chase the squirrel (fact 16 via rule 12).\n\nNow, with the cow chasing the squirrel (fact 16), are there any other rules?\n\nLooking at rule 13: If something eats the squirrel and the squirrel eats the mouse, then the mouse chases the cow.\n\nThe first part: something eats the squirrel.The bear does that (fact 2).The second part: the squirrel eats the mouse.Is that the case?The squirrel\'s actions are eating the bear (statement 11).There\'s no mention of the squirrel eating the mouse.Therefore, the second condition isn\'t met, so rule 13\'s antecedent isn\'t satisfied.\n\nWait, but maybe the squirrel eats the mouse via some other inference?Let me check all the facts and inferences again.The squirrel eats the bear (statement 11), and that\'s it.The mouse is eaten by the cow (fact 6).So no, the squirrel doesn\'t eat the mouse.Therefore, rule 13\'s antecedent isn\'t met.\n\nNow, let\'s see if any other rules can be applied.\n\nRule 18: We already used it to get the bear eating the cow.\n\nRule 19: Already used to get the mouse visiting the bear.\n\nRule 20: Already used to get the bear blue.\n\nNow, the mouse visiting the bear (fact 17) might be important.Let me check if that affects any other rules.For instance, if the mouse visits the bear, and if the mouse also visits the mouse, but that\'s not stated.Alternatively, does any other condition require that?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the mouse\'s visit to the bear (fact 17) plus some other action could trigger another rule, but I don\'t see it immediately.\n\nThe mouse does not chase the bear.</think>.\n\nThe problem requires determining if the statement "The mouse does not chase the bear" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context and conditional rules. Here\'s the step-by-step analysis:.\n\n### Key Inferences:.\n1. **Rule 15**: Since the cow eats the mouse (