e\'s furry?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can\'t determine that.So perhaps the answer is unknown?\n\nWait, but let me think again.Let me check all the given info again to see if I missed something.\n\nLooking at the premises again:\n\nPremise 5: All kind things are furry.\n\nPremise 8: If furry, then green.\n\nPremise 7: If cold, then kind.\n\nPremise 12: All round, cold things are rough.Since Bob is round but not cold, that doesn\'t apply.\n\nPremise 10: Round things are not quiet.So Bob isn\'t quiet.\n\nPremise 9: If Dave is quiet, then Dave isn\'t furry.But Dave is rough (premise 3), and if Dave is quiet, then by premise 11: if quiet and not kind, then not rough.Wait, Dave is rough, so if he were quiet and not kind, that would be a contradiction.But Dave is green and rough.Let me see, but that\'s a side note.\n\nBack to Bob.Since we can\'t determine if Bob is furry, we can\'t confirm he\'s kind.But can we infer anything else?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to show that Bob must be furry.Let me see.\n\nWait, premise 8 says if furry → green.But we need the converse.Since Bob is green, does that imply he must be furry?No, because the premise is one-way.So being green is necessary for being furry, but not sufficient.So there might be green things that aren\'t furry.\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that requires all green things to be furry?Let me check all premises again.No, only that green things are round (premise 6).So no, so Bob could be green and not furry.Therefore, we can\'t assume he is furry.\n\nTherefore, since we don\'t know if Bob is furry, we can\'t confirm he is kind.But the question is asking whether "Bob is not kind" is true, false, or unknown.So if it\'s possible that Bob is kind, then the statement "Bob is not kind" would be false.But if there\'s no way he can be kind, then the statement is true.If it\'s possible either way, then it\'s unknown.\n\nWait, let\'s consider the possibility of Bob being kind.To be kind, he must be either cold (he isn\'t) or be furry (since premise 5 requires kind to be furry).Since he could be furry, but we don\'t know, so there\'s a possibility he is kind (if he\'s furry) or not (if he\'s not).Therefore, we can\'t determine for sure.Therefore, the statement "Bob is not kind" could be true or false, so the answer would be unknown (C).\n\nWait, but let me check again.Let me think of another angle.Since all kind things are furry, but being furry doesn\'t necessarily make you kind.However, are there any other constraints?For instance, if something is furry, is there any other requirement to be kind?\n\nAlternatively, maybe through premise 7, but that\'s only for cold things.So the only way to be kind is either being cold or being furry (but not necessarily).Wait no, premise 5 says all kind things are furry, but being furry is necessary but not sufficient.So being kind requires being furry, but there might be other conditions.Since there\'s no other condition stated, maybe being furry is sufficient?Wait no, the premises don\'t say that.The premises only say that being kind requires being furry, but being furry doesn\'t guarantee being kind.Therefore, there might be furry things that aren\'t kind.Therefore, Bob could be furry but not kind, or not furry and not kind.Therefore, we can\'t know.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Bob is not kind" could be true or false depending on whether he is furry or not.Since we don\'t have information about his furrieness, the answer should be unknown (C).Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, let me see if there\'s any other rule that can link furrieness to other properties of Bob.Since Bob is green and round, maybe through other rules.\n\nWait, premise 8: furry implies green, which he is, so that\'s okay.But there\'s no other rule that links roundness or greenness to furrieness.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through the contrapositive of premise 8: if not green, then not furry.But since Bob is green, that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe if something is round and furry, does that lead to anything?Not that I can see.\n\nHmm.Alternatively, let\'s think of the question again: Is "Bob is not kind" true, false, or unknown.\n\nIf we can prove Bob is kind, then the statement is false.If we can prove he\'s not kind, then the statement is true.Otherwise, it\'s unknown.\n\nWe can\'t prove he\'s kind, since we don\'t know if he\'s furry.But can we prove he\'s not kind?\n\nSuppose we can show that Bob cannot be furry.If he can\'t be furry, then by premise 5, he can\'t be kind, so the statement "Bob is not kind" would be true.\n\nWait, can we show Bob is not furry?\n\nLet me think.Suppose Bob is not furry.Then by premise 8, he is not green (which he isn\'t).But are there any contradictions?\n\nWait, if Bob is not furry and kind, then that\'s not okay.But if he is not furry but kind, that\'s not allowed.Since the premises don\'t say that all non-furry things are kind.The only thing is that kind things must not be furry.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if there\'s a rule that would make not being furry and round lead to something else.For example, premise 10 says round things are quiet.But that doesn\'t connect to non-furrieness.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is not furry and kind, then since he\'s kind, he would have to be either not cold (he isn\'t) or not just furry (which is not allowed via premise 5).Wait, but premise 5 is kind→not furry, not the other way around.So not being furry doesn\'t make you kind.So Bob could be not furry and kind, or not furry and not kind.Since there\'s no rule that requires non-furry things to be kind unless they are not cold, which he isn\'t.So there\'s no contradiction in Bob being not furry and kind.Therefore, we can\'t rule out the possibility that Bob is not furry and thus kind, but we can\'t confirm it either.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no way to determine if Bob is not furry, we can\'t determine his kind status.Therefore, the statement "Bob is kind" could be true or false, so the answer is unknown (C).Hmm, but wait, maybe I missed something.\n\nWait, let\'s check if there\'s any other rule that could affect this.Let me think again.\n\nPremise 11: If something is quiet and not kind, then it is not rough.Since Bob is round, premise 10 says he is quiet.So Bob can be quiet, so the antecedent (quiet and not kind) can be true for Bob.Therefore, this premise doesn\'t affect Bob.\n\nAnother thought: Since all kind things are not furry (premise 5), and all not furry things are not green (premise 8).So kind things must not be green.Bob is not green, so that\'s okay.But that doesn\'t mean he\'s kind.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that says all green things are not kind?No, that\'s not stated.Only that green things are round (premise 6).\n\nHmm.So I think I have to conclude that it\'s unknown.But wait, let me check the options again.The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here.Let me see another angle.Let me try to see if Bob must not be kind or not.\n\nWait, premise 7 says if something is cold, then it is kind.Fiona is not cold, so Fiona is not kind.That\'s clear.\n\nNow, for Bob, since he is not green and round.Let me see if there\'s any rule that requires a not green, not round thing to be kind.Let\'s see.\n\nGreen things are round (premise 6), so that\'s not satisfied.But no rule says green round things are not kind.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through the non-furrieness.If Bob is not furry, he could not be kind, but we don\'t know.\n\nWait, let\'s suppose that Bob is not furry.Then he could not be kind, but there\'s no rule that says all not furry things are not kind.So he could be not furry and not kind, or not furry and kind.Since we don\'t know, so we can\'t say.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path.Let me think of all the premises again.\n\nPremise 5: All kind → not furry.\n\nPremise 8: not furry → not green.\n\nPremise 7: cold → kind.\n\nPremise 12: round and cold → rough.\n\nPremise 10: round → quiet.\n\nPremise 9: If Dave is quiet → not furry.\n\nPremise 11: quiet and not kind → not rough.\n\nHmm.Let me consider if there\'s any way that not being round and not green (Bob\'s traits) could lead to not being kind.Since non-roundness and non-greenness don\'t directly imply anything else related to kind except via non-furrieness.\n\nAlternatively, since all kind things are not furry, and all not furry things are not green, then kind things are a subset of not green things.But that doesn\'t mean all not green things are kind.\n\nTherefore, Bob could be in the subset of not green things that are kind.Therefore, the statement "Bob is kind" could be true, but it\'s possible he is not kind.Since we can\'t determine, it\'s unknown.\n\nWait, but hold on.Let me think of another angle.Since all kind things are not furry, and all not furry things are not green, but there\'s no rule that requires a not green thing to be not furry.Therefore, there can be not green things that are furry, hence kind.So Bob could not be one of those, so the statement "Bob is kind" would be true.But also, Bob could not be furry and kind, so it\'s possible either way.Since there\'s no information to confirm or deny his non-furrieness, the answer is unknown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules implicitly require something else.Let me see if there\'s any other connection.\n\nWait, since Bob is not round (premise 2), and premise 10 says round things are quiet.But that doesn\'t affect non-furrieness.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is not furry, then he\'s not kind (by contrapositive of premise 5).So if he\'s not furry, then the statement "Bob is kind" is true.If he is furry, then he could not be kind or not.Wait, but if he\'s furry, he can not be kind only if he meets some other condition.The only way to not be kind is being cold (which he isn\'t) or being furry?But premise 5 is only a one-way implication.So not being furry is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nWait, maybe there\'s another rule that requires