al statements:\n\n12.Big people are round.So if someone is big, they must be round.\n\n13.All blue people are not round.So blue people cannot be round.\n\n14.If someone is big and round then they are green.So (big ∧ round) → green.\n\n15.If Bob is not red then Bob is not big.So ¬Red(Bob) → ¬Big(Bob).\n\n16.If Bob is round then Bob is big.Round(Bob) → Big(Bob).\n\n17.Red people are kind.So Red → Kind.Therefore, since Bob is red (statement7), Bob must be kind.But wait, the context says "Red people are kind", so Bob is red → Bob is kind.But wait, in the context, Anne is not kind (statement4), but that\'s okay because Anne is not red.\n\nWait, but the problem is that in the context, there is a statement about Bob being round (statement8).So let\'s see if that leads to any contradictions.\n\nFirst, Bob is round (statement8).Then, according to statement16, if Bob is round, then he must be big.So Bob must be big.Therefore, Bob is big.\n\nThen, since Bob is big, by statement12 (big people are round), which is okay because Bob is already round, so that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, since Bob is big and round (from statements8 and derived big), then by statement14 (if someone is big and round, they are green), which aligns with Bob\'s green attribute (statement6).So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, Bob is red (statement7).By statement17, red people are kind, so Bob must be kind.However, the question is whether that\'s okay.Wait, but there\'s another rule: "Kind people are big."(statement20).Since Bob is kind (from being red), then he must be big, which we already have from the roundness.So that\'s okay.\n\nBut wait, there\'s also statement19: If someone is kind and green then they are furry.Since Bob is kind (from red) and green (statement6), then he must be furry.Which he is (statement5).So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, let\'s check for any contradictions.\n\nIs there any rule that would contradict Bob being round?\n\nLet\'s see.Another rule is statement13: All blue people are not round.Since Bob is not stated to be blue, that\'s okay.Erin is not blue (statement9), but Bob\'s color isn\'t mentioned except green, red, but being green and red doesn\'t mean he can\'t be blue?Wait, but colors are attributes.Wait, in the context, the properties are listed as "Anne is green", "Bob is green", etc.So does a person have only one color or multiple?The way it\'s written, Bob is green, red, and round.So perhaps colors are additive?So Bob is both green and red.But then, is there a rule about conflicting colors?Not that I can see.The only color-related rules are:\n\n- All blue people are not round (so if someone is blue, they can\'t be round.But Bob isn\'t stated to be blue, so that\'s okay).\n\n- Red people are kind.So that\'s okay.\n\nNow, looking at Gary\'s statements.Gary is not furry (statement11).The rule 18 says if Gary is not blue then he is furry.Since Gary is not furry, the contrapositive would be: if Gary is not furry, then he must be blue.So Gary is blue.Therefore, Gary is blue.But Erin is not blue (statement9).But that\'s okay.So Gary must be blue.But that\'s a conclusion from the premises, not a contradiction.\n\nNow, back to Bob.The only possible issue could be with his being red and kind.Wait, but that\'s okay.Since he is red, he must be kind, and then kind people are big, so he\'s big, which we already have.So all that checks out.\n\nTherefore, there are no contradictions in the premises as given.Given the lack of evidence that contradicts the statement, it is clear that Bob is not round, as the context unequivocally suggests otherwise.</think>.\n\nA) True.\n\nThe context explicitly states "Bob is round" as a direct fact (statement 8). All other premises are consistent with this assertion. For example:.\n- Bob being round requires him to be big (via statement 16), which aligns with the rule that big people are round (statement 12).\n- His red color makes him kind (statement 17), and since kind people are big (statement 20), this further confirms 