e, the cow isn\'t red, regardless of whether the bear eats the lion or not.\n\nAh!This is a key point I missed earlier.\n\nThe lion doesn\'t see the cow (because it doesn\'t, as per its not being red and not eating itself), and the cow doesn\'t eat the squirrel.\n\nTherefore, by statement 11, the cow must not be red.\n\nTherefore, the cow isn\'t red.\n\nThis is a necessary conclusion, not dependent on the bear\'s actions.\n\nTherefore, the cow isn\'t red.\n\nNow, since the cow isn\'t red, by statement 16, the cow must not eat the lion.\n\nSo, the cow doesn\'t eat the lion.\n\nTherefore, the cow not eating the lion is a necessary conclusion.\n\nNow, the question is whether the bear eats the lion.\n\nIf the cow must not eat the lion (because it\'s not red), then does that interfere with the bear\'s ability not to eat the lion?\n\nNo, multiple animals can\'t eat the same prey.\n\nBut, wait, the problem now requires that the cow must not eat the lion.\n\nTherefore, the cow doesn\'t eat the lion.\n\nAdditionally, the bear might or might not eat the lion.\n\nHowever, if the bear doesn\'t eat the lion, then by statement 18, the bear must not see the cow.\n\nIs there any contradiction if the bear does see the cow?\n\nIf the bear eats the lion, then statement 18 doesn\'t require it to not see the cow.\n\nBut the cow isn\'t red because the lion doesn\'t see it.\n\nTherefore, the cow not being red is already established, and the cow doesn\'t eat the lion (statement 16).\n\nTherefore, the bear\'s not eating of the lion is optional?\n\nWait, no.The cow must not eat the lion because it\'s not red.\n\nThe bear\'s not eating the lion is an extra action that\'s not required but possible unless prohibited.\n\nTherefore, the bear could choose not to eat the lion, but it\'s not necessary.\n\nHowever, the question is whether the statement "The bear doesn\'t eat the lion" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nWe have to determine if it\'s necessarily true (so the answer is A), necessarily false