ung things are round. So roundness is necessary for youth, but not sufficient. So unless there's another rule that requires round + something else to be young.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that says if something is kind and round, then it's young? No, that's not stated.\n\nFact 9 says round things are kind, so all round things are kind, but not necessarily vice versa. So being kind doesn't imply roundness unless stated.\n\nWait, so Gary is round, so he must be kind (fact 9). Which is already given (he is kind), so that's consistent.\n\nSo there's no rule that requires a round (hence kind) thing to be young unless they have another attribute (red), which Gary doesn't have.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that there's no information to determine if Gary is young. Thus, the answer should be unknown (C).\n\nWait, but wait another thought. Let me check the contrapositive of some rules.\n\nSuppose Gary is young, then he must be round (fact 12). Since he is round, that's okay. But being young would require other things? For example, if he were young, then according to fact 12, he is round (which is true). But does being young require any other attributes?\n\nLooking at other rules, like fact 13: Kind and red things are young. But that's about being young if you're kind and red. Not about being young leading to something else.\n\nAlternatively, fact 14 says red and round things are young. Again, if something is red and round, it's young. But Gary isn't red.\n\nAlternatively, if Gary were young, would that mean he must be red? There's no such rule. So, if Gary is young, he just needs to be round (which he is), but there's no requirement for him to have other attributes unless specified.\n\nTherefore, since there's no rule that requires round and kind to be young, and no rule that prohibits a round, kind, green thing from being non-young, we can't conclude either way. So the answer is unknown.\n\nWait, but hold on. The problem says \"If something is round and quiet then it is green\" (fact15). Gary is round and quiet, so he must be green. So he's green. Now, is there any rule that connects green and kind to youth?\n\nNo. So I think my original conclusion holds. The correct answer is C) Unknown.\n\nBut let me check if I made a mistake in assuming that \"round implies kind\" (fact9). Yes, so Gary is kind because he's round, but that doesn't give any more info.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a chain of inferences I missed.\n\nWait, let's consider all the attributes Gary has:\n\n- Round (fact7)\n- Kind (fact6, also from fact9)\n- Quiet (from fact11)\n- Green (from fact15)\n\nNow, is there a rule that says green things have to be young? No.\n\nIs there a rule that says quiet things are young? Not directly. Fact11 says round \u2192 quiet, but quietness alone doesn't imply anything else unless combined with another attribute (like roundness and quietness leading to green).\n\nAlternatively, fact15 is already applied.\n\nHmm, I think I've considered all angles. The key is that while Gary has several attributes, none of them combine to require him to be young, nor is there any rule that would contradict him not being young. So the answer is unknown.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{C}",
