that the antecedent is true or false, then we could get something.But since we don\'t know if the squirrel visits the rabbit, and we don\'t know if the rabbit likes the bear, this might not help directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path.\n\nLooking back at the premises, is there any other conditional that could involve the rabbit liking the bear?\n\nStatement 15 says if something likes the bear and the bear likes the rabbit, then the bear is green.Wait, the bear does like the rabbit (from earlier conclusion), and the something that likes the bear is the squirrel (statement 9).So the antecedent of 15 is already true (since squirrel likes bear and bear likes rabbit).Therefore, the conclusion that the bear is green is already satisfied, so that\'s consistent with our previous conclusion.\n\nBut how does that help us with the rabbit\'s feelings?\n\nHmm.Let me think again.The question is about the rabbit\'s liking of the bear.Let\'s see if any of the conditionals can be triggered by that.\n\nSuppose the rabbit does like the bear.Then, according to statement 16, if the squirrel also visits the rabbit, then the bear would visit the squirrel.But we don\'t know if the squirrel visits the rabbit, so that might not help.Alternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction if we assume the rabbit doesn\'t like the bear?\n\nAlternatively, is there any other condition that requires the rabbit to like the bear?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can look at other relationships.The rabbit chases the bear (statement 6).Does chasing imply any liking?Not that I can see from the premises.The only implications are about chasing leading to color, or liking leading to chasing, etc.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only way to determine if the rabbit likes the bear is through some other conditional.\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s any other condition that involves the rabbit liking someone.Let me check all the conditionals again:\n\nStatement 12: If X likes the lion → X chases squirrel.\n\nIf 