irectly contradicted by this.But maybe there\'s some inconsistency or something that overrides this?Let me check all the premises again to see if there\'s any conflict.\n\nFirst, let\'s note down all the facts about Gary:\n\n- Gary is not blue (point 3)\n- Gary is kind (point 4)\n- Gary is not rough (point 5)\n- Gary is not young (point 6)\n\nSo from the given statements, Gary is explicitly stated to be kind.So why is the question asking if "Gary is not kind" is true?That seems contradictory.Unless there\'s a rule that could make this false?Wait, maybe there\'s a contradiction in the premises?\n\nWait, let me check each rule again.Let me see if any of the rules could lead to a contradiction with the given facts.\n\nFirst, Gary is not blue (point 3).Then, the eighth premise says "If Gary is green then Gary is not blue."Since Gary is not blue, that conditional is true regardless of whether he is green.So that doesn\'t add anything new.\n\nGary is not young (point 6).The ninth premise says all young people are blue.Since Gary isn\'t young, that doesn\'t apply to him.\n\nGary\'s kindness is directly stated (point 4).Is there any rule that could contradict that?Let\'s see the other rules:\n\nRule 14: If someone is blue and white, then they are kind.But Gary isn\'t blue, so this doesn\'t affect him.\n\nRule 15: All red people are not rough.Since Gary isn\'t rough (point 5), that\'s okay, but doesn\'t relate to kindness.\n\nRule 13: If someone is green, then they are white.If Gary were green, he would have to be white, but that\'s not conflicting with his kindness.But is Gary green?Let\'s see.\n\nWait, do we know Gary\'s color?From point 3, Gary isn\'t blue.The problem doesn\'t say Gary\'s color directly except that he isn\'t blue.The other color mentions: Harry is green (point7).The rules also mention that all red people are green (point11).So if someone is red, they must be green.\n\nAlso, blue young people are red (point12).Since Gary isn\'t young (point6), this doesn\'t apply to him.\n\nIs there any rule that could determine Gary\'s color?Let me see.The problem states that Gary is not blue (point3), but we don\'t know if he\'s red, green, white, etc.However, the question is about his kindness, not color, so maybe that\'s irrelevant here.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any possible contradiction in the premises.For instance, if Gary is kind (point4), and rule 14 says if someone is blue and white, they are kind.But Gary isn\'t blue, so that\'s okay.So his kindness is given, so the statement "Gary is not kind" must be false.So the answer would be B (False) because the statement is false.\n\nBut wait, maybe there\'s a hidden contradiction.Let me check again.\n\nWait, let me consider all the premises again to make sure there\'s no conflict with the given facts.For example:\n\n- Gary is not young (6), so he doesn\'t have to be blue (since all young are blue, but he\'s not young).That\'s okay.\n\n- Since Gary is not young, the 16th rule (if young and not white, then rough) doesn\'t apply to him.Since he\'s not young, his being rough or not is only covered by point5 (he\'s not rough), which is okay.\n\n- The 15th rule says all red people are not rough.Since Gary is rough, he must be red, but he must also not be red.But there\'s info that says he is red.Since he\'s rough, being red is not allowed, but required.So conflict here.\n\n- The 13th rule says if someone is green, they are white.If Gary were green, he wouldn\'t have to be white, but since we know his color, that\'s not okay.But again, since his kindness is directly stated, that\'s not separate.\n\nWait, maybe the solution is that in the premises, there\'s a rule that might make someone kind even if they are not kind?Let me check the rules again.\n\nThe rules that mention kindness are:\n\n- Rule 14: Blue and white implies kind.\n\n- Gary is not kind (point4).\n\nThere\'s no rule that says someone can be kind unless some condition.The only way to contradict point4 is if there